<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angkor Borei</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> Borei is a town and archaeological site in Takeo province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, near the Vietnamese border. Excavations have revealed brick walls, canals linking it to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oc-eo">Oc Eo</a>, and occupation from the late 1st millennium BCE, and many scholars see it as a major centre, perhaps a capital, of Funan. It has also yielded important early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-borei.jpg" alt="Angkor Borei" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Fussan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Borei_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The people lived in stilt houses, grew rice and sent tribute of gold, silver, ivory and exotic animals; a canal system linked <b>Angkor Borei</b> to Óc Eo and the coast, and an elaborate system of water storage and irrigation supported fishing and rice along the delta's rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
